@miretskiy it's an interesting issue, if restore is consistently hitting the newTimer codepath that means that the checksum computations are being throttled by the limiter. Looking at the rate.Limiter code it looks like the go authors chose to use a Timer instead of Sleep to allow for the wait to terminate early if the context is cancelled or expired. 

It seems that an interesting avenue to explore would be to see if we can disable or otherwise reduce the consistency_queue checker, since all of those sha512 calculations are also creating a lot of pressure on the CPU. Looking at the code the checker is going to run as soon as it can for a range and then every 24 hours. A potential change would be to introduce an offset to the scheduler, so rather than scheduling a consistency check immediately for a range it can do so at a random offset between 0 and the checker interval (i.e. 24 hours by default). 

